# AI Intelligence Hub — Permanent Design Rules
# These rules apply to EVERY page and component in this project.

## RTL (Right-to-Left) Layout
- The entire application is in Hebrew → direction is always RTL.
- `<html dir="rtl">` and `direction: rtl` in globals.css are the single source of truth.
- Never add `dir="ltr"` to a container unless it wraps purely English/code content (e.g., <select> with English option labels is acceptable).

## Sidebar Placement
- The Sidebar must ALWAYS be on the RIGHT side of the screen.
- Use `flex-row-reverse` on the outermost layout wrapper.
- Put the main content area FIRST in the DOM, then the Sidebar — `flex-row-reverse` will push the Sidebar to the right visually.
- Example structure:
    <div className="flex flex-row-reverse h-screen w-full overflow-hidden">
      <main>…content…</main>
      <Sidebar />          ← second in DOM, appears on RIGHT
    </div>

## Text Alignment
- All Hebrew text is right-aligned by default (inherited from `text-align: right` on body).
- Use `text-start` (not `text-right`) for Tailwind alignment so it respects direction automatically.
- Use `text-end` for labels that should align to the LEFT in RTL context.

## Icons & Arrows
- "Back" navigation buttons use `ChevronRight` (→) because in RTL, right = the starting/home direction.
- "Forward" / "next" buttons use `ChevronLeft` (←).
- Collapse toggles: use `ChevronLeft` for "collapse left", `ChevronRight` for "expand right".

## Borders
- A sidebar on the RIGHT uses `border-l` (physical left border = the divider between content and sidebar).
- Headers and footers use `border-b` / `border-t` as normal.

## New Pages Checklist
Before shipping any new page, verify:
[ ] Outer layout wrapper uses `flex flex-row-reverse` (if it has a sidebar or panel).
[ ] Sidebar / panel appears on the RIGHT.
[ ] No accidental `dir="ltr"` on Hebrew text containers.
[ ] Back-navigation icon is ChevronRight.
[ ] Text is right-aligned without explicit `text-right` overrides (should be automatic).
